WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:02.460
Ian Phillips: The average
diameter of human hair

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:05.820
is 17 to 181 micrometers.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:07.211
Not centimeters;

00:00:07.211 --> 00:00:08.769
not millimeters;

00:00:08.769 --> 00:00:10.144
micrometers.

00:00:10.144 --> 00:00:12.450
And whether they're applied
to a prosthetic piece

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.840
or woven in, movie makeup artists

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.690
have to painstakingly
apply each micro hair

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:20.720
to create something as realistic as this,

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.450
Kurt Russell's beard in
"Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2."

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:25.540
In some of these shots,

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:27.830
Kurt is sporting his real beard,

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:29.640
while in others it's a fake.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.190
Can't tell the difference?

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:32.560
Neither can I,

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.110
and neither can this guy, John Blake,

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:38.500
the makeup artist who made
the fake beard for the movie.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.580
Hollywood makeup artists
have to make facial hair

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:43.270
so convincing that even they're not sure

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:45.210
what's real in the end.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.060
There are four methods makeup artists

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:48.950
use to apply fake facial hair,

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:50.720
all of which have to look real.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.490
Three types are applied
onto an actor's face,

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:55.290
and the fourth type is actually applied

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.670
to a hyperreal prosthetic head.

00:00:57.670 --> 00:00:59.980
The benefit of working with a prosthesis

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:01.680
is that you can make the hair look

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:05.040
as if it's coming straight
from the skin, like real hair.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.130
You do this by punching it in.

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:09.163
But punching those fine hairs

00:01:09.163 --> 00:01:11.546
is a time-consuming process.

00:01:11.546 --> 00:01:14.240
For example, to get eyelashes right,

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.210
a makeup artist like Kenneth Calhoun

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:18.630
takes a long strand of hair and bends it.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:19.740
He then takes a needle

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:22.410
and punches two eyelashes in at a time.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:25.390
After the few hours it
takes to punch in the hairs,

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:27.600
he focuses on the details.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:28.950
Kenneth Calhoun: You can't
just put a bunch of lashes

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:29.883
going out straight.

00:01:29.883 --> 00:01:31.970
They don't stick out
like that; they fan out.

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:33.250
Ian: Along with fanning out,

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:35.420
real lashes have a curl to them.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:36.800
To get a distinct bend,

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.180
he uses his needle to wrap the
hairs around a heated iron.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:43.560
You can see how they
both fan out and curl.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.550
Kenneth punched stubble into a fake head

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:48.090
when working on the
2020 film "The Rental."

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:49.910
But when working with a prosthesis,

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:52.990
giving it the illusion of hair
can be almost as realistic

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:54.660
as punching in the real thing.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:56.340
For this prosthetic head,

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.500
Kenneth painted a mix of grays on the neck

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.260
that perfectly gives the
appearance of shadow and stubble

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.400
with no hairs applied.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.000
The second way is to use the lace method,

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.900
probably the most commonly used method

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:10.310
to apply fake hair to an actor's face.

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:11.471
John Blake: Here's your piece of lace.

00:02:11.471 --> 00:02:14.180
It's made out of nylon,
and it has little holes,

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:18.530
and a little hook is used
to tie each individual hair.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:20.850
Ian: Like punching
hairs into a prosthesis,

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:22.870
the lace method is labor intensive

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:25.130
because each hair is added with a needle.

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:27.650
The difference? Instead of punching it in,

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:29.460
it's threaded through holes.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.900
Here, makeup artist Kristy Staky

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:33.734
is tying an especially long beard

00:02:33.734 --> 00:02:35.810
for the show "Dwight in Shining Armor."

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:37.240
At the top of the beard,

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.510
she only ties one hair per hole.

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:42.400
Farther down, she ties
more hairs to a hole,

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:43.970
but spreads them out.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:46.710
If she did one hair per hole
throughout the whole piece,

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:49.241
it would get too dense and then look fake.

00:02:49.241 --> 00:02:52.890
Lace pieces often take up to
six days to knot and style,

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:54.140
and the average lace beard

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:57.050
contains 200 hairs per square inch.

00:02:57.050 --> 00:02:59.041
It's why John Blake needed to work with

00:02:59.041 --> 00:03:00.291
four other makeup artists

00:03:00.291 --> 00:03:02.472
to tie Kurt Russell's fake beard together

00:03:02.472 --> 00:03:04.120
in a pinch for "Guardians 2"

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:06.990
after the actor had to shave
his real beard for a flashback

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:09.260
and didn't have time to grow it back.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:11.100
Because all the hairs are tied in,

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.420
lace pieces stay tight on the face,

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:15.370
making them ideal for action movies

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:18.150
and even more ideal for
blending into the skin.

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:20.010
This is why you might've never guessed

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:21.560
that Captain Barbossa's beard

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:24.130
in the "Pirates of the
Caribbean" franchise was fake.

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:27.020
It's one of John Blake's
many masterpieces.

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:29.640
Still, when working on a lace piece,

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:31.288
it's not as simple as recreating

00:03:31.288 --> 00:03:33.580
how an actor's hair would naturally grow.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:35.480
There's a ton of creativity involved,

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:37.960
depending on what the script calls for.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:40.000
For "Captain America: Civil War,"

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.310
John made these eyebrows

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.772
for William Hurt to play Thunderbolt Ross.

00:03:43.772 --> 00:03:46.131
John: He had to be a military man,

00:03:46.131 --> 00:03:48.930
and he wanted to have a
more hawkish-like quality,

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:50.520
and to create this hawkish-like quality

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:52.590
we created false eyebrows.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:53.900
Ian: To get the right effect,

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.180
choosing the right type of hair was key.

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:58.230
Yak hair, which is thick and coarse,

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:00.620
is commonly used to replicate beards,

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:02.610
but eyebrows are so thin and delicate

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:05.050
that John went with angora hair instead,

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:06.840
which is much smaller in diameter

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.040
but still made the actor's
eyebrows look thicker

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.550
than his real-life brows.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:13.590
Where lace-applied fake facial hair

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:15.510
can leave an actor feeling stiff,

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:18.530
the third method lets an actor
move their face more freely.

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:20.040
It's called hand laying.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:23.440
John C. Reilly is wearing a
hand-laid beard in "Walk Hard,"

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:26.110
and you can see just how expressive he is.

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:27.300
For this method,

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.070
makeup artists apply
glue to the actor's skin

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:31.640
and place the hair in clumps

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:33.620
straight onto the performer's face.

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:35.303
By putting the clumps of loose hair

00:04:35.303 --> 00:04:37.353
between the thumb and forefinger

00:04:37.353 --> 00:04:40.210
and dragging it back
and forth on the glue,

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:42.650
John can even leave natural stubble behind

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:44.340
on the cheeks and jawline.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:46.690
Hand laying hair is
also the best technique

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:48.200
if you want to add more hair

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:51.070
to an actor's already
existing facial hair.

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:52.690
The key is hiding the line between

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:54.520
where the real facial hair ends

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:56.290
and where the fake hair starts,

00:04:56.290 --> 00:04:58.410
like the makeup artist
for "Silicon Valley"

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:01.700
had to do to actor Martin
Starr for the series finale.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:05.080
To make sure the line between
real and fake was invisible,

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:06.780
he curled the hairs in and out

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:09.210
until it looked like this.

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:11.710
To show the fourth and final method,

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:14.920
we asked Kenneth to
recreate, well, my beard.

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:17.010
So, this is about the longest
I'll let my beard grow

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:19.050
before it really needs a trim.

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:21.250
Kenneth is going to use
the floating method,

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:23.830
which is great for actors
who aren't on set every day,

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:26.030
because this type of hairpiece only lasts

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:28.110
for about one or two applications.

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:29.540
To make a floated beard,

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:31.765
Kenneth forms the beard on a fake head,

00:05:31.765 --> 00:05:33.565
then hairsprays it.

00:05:33.565 --> 00:05:37.500
Then the whole piece gets
glued onto an actor's face.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:39.400
Even though the process is simpler,

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:41.570
it still requires attention to detail.

00:05:41.570 --> 00:05:43.160
Kenneth: There are a few
things that I noticed,

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:45.940
was just the coarseness
underneath the chin.

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:47.170
Something else I noticed is, like,

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:48.910
the direction of your sideburns,

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:51.150
how they kind of swirl as they go down.

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:52.810
You have, like, a couple of parts here

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:53.957
where your mustache grows down,

00:05:53.957 --> 00:05:56.200
and you have a second area
that grows down as well.

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:57.990
Ian: To match the different consistencies

00:05:57.990 --> 00:05:59.100
of my facial hair,

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:02.420
Kenneth used a combination
of yak hair and crepe wool.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:03.920
The crepe wool is so curly

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:06.360
that it needs to bathe
in warm water first.

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:08.920
You can make a lot more
shapes with the crepe wool,

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:11.260
so it's better for the
curlier parts of the beard,

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:13.880
while the yak hair is better
for the straighter hairs.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:16.130
And like any piece of real facial hair,

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:19.020
there's a lot of grooming
and styling along the way.

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:20.980
Now, the big reveal.

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:22.850
Oh, my God.
Kenneth: You like it?

00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:25.130
Ian: Looks exactly like mine.

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:26.390
I could wear that on my face,

00:06:26.390 --> 00:06:29.390
and I think people would not
be able to tell the difference.

